<description>
Fill each plus-shaped background notch with the color of the frame side it opens toward.

Input structure
- A one-cell-thick rectangular frame has four solid-colored sides and neutral corner pixels.
- The frame interior contains a dominant background color and one foreground object color.
- Some object boundaries contain five-pixel plus-shaped background notches.

Rule
- Find every background plus whose opposite side is supported by foreground object pixels and whose open side faces one frame side.
- Recolor the five pixels of that plus with the color of the facing frame side.
- Leave all other pixels unchanged.

Key insight
The frame side colors are direction labels for the open notches in the foreground object.
</description>
<annotation>
{
  "type": "transform_in_place",
  "src": "solid-color rectangular frame whose four side colors label directions; monochrome 4-connected foreground objects on the dominant background with 5-pixel plus-shaped background notches",
  "transform": "for each plus-shaped background notch, determine its open side from the adjacent foreground support and recolor the five plus pixels to the matching frame side color; leave all other pixels unchanged"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
spec:
  grid: 30x30 [observed]
  frame:
    border: 1-cell-thick rectangle; 4 corners are color 0
    sides: top/bottom/left/right each a distinct solid color; all 4 differ from corner, bg, and obj (7 distinct colors total)
  interior:
    bg: dominant color filling the region inside the frame
    obj: single foreground color forming 3-4 4-connected blob components
  objects:
    count: 3-4
    shape: roughly rectangular blob; each edge is either straight or has exactly one depth-2 V-notch
    bbox: each dimension 5-20; area 28-228 [observed]
    notches_per_object: 1-3; at most 1 notch per edge
    separation: >=2 cells between any two objects; >=1 cell from frame
  notch:
    structure: 5-pixel plus (cross) of bg cells carved into an object edge
    pattern: edge profile is [...,0,1,2,1,0,...] deviation from baseline (depth always 2)
    position: notch apex >=2 cells from the nearest corner of that edge
    direction: the open arm of the plus faces one of the 4 frame sides
    fill_rule: in output, the 5 bg cells are recolored to the color of the facing frame side
  total_notches: 3-8

generation_strategy:
  approach: output-first
  steps:
    1. Pick 7 distinct colors for corner(0), 4 sides, bg, obj.
    2. Draw the 30x30 frame (corner pixels, solid side rows/cols).
    3. Place 3-4 rectangular blobs of obj color in the interior, ensuring >=2 cell gaps and >=1 cell from frame.
    4. For each blob, choose 1-3 edges to receive a notch (at most 1 per edge). Pick a position >=2 from edge corners. Assign a direction matching that edge (top edge -> top, etc.).
    5. Carve each V-notch into the blob edge: remove the depth-2 staircase pattern [...,0,1,2,1,0,...] from the obj, creating 5 bg cells in a plus shape.
    6. Verify each notch's open arm faces bg (not another object) and the closed arm has obj support. The result is the input grid.
    7. To produce the output, fill each 5-cell plus with the color of its facing frame side.
```
</generation_spec>
